/** \page cp-config Config

The `Marmalade::Config` API is a powerful API to manage configuration files, and update them between versions.

\section basics Basics

We'll go by example, generating a basic config file.

```cpp
using namespace Marmalade;

// ----- myconfig.h -----

// Structure representing the config
// Default values can be placed inside curly brackets
struct MyConfig {
    std::string name{"Hello"};
    int count{5};
    std::vector<std::string> items{};
};

// Class for MyConfig
class MyConfigObj : public Config<MyConfig> {
    public:
        explicit MyConfigObj();

        // Declare a singleton
        CONFIG_SINGLETON(MyConfigObj)

        // Declare a getter for easy config access
        CONFIG_DECL_GET_FUNC(MyConfigObj)
};

// Define JSON type for MyConfig
NLOHMANN_DEFINE_TYPE_NON_INTRUSIVE(MyConfig, name, count);

// ----- myconfig.cpp -----

MyConfigObj::MyConfigObj() : Config<MyConfig>(ConfigUtil::GetConfigDirectory() / "myconfig.json") {
    // Enable fancy GUI error handling
    useGui = true;
}
```

That is all that is required for basic config. For usage:

```cpp
// Pass config error dialog for fancy GUI error handling
MyConfig::GetInstance().LoadConfig(&GUI::WindowManager::GetInstance().configErrorDlg);

// Accessing data:
std::cout << MyConfig::GetStoredConfig().name << std::endl;
MyConfig::GetStoredConfig().name = "New name";

// Saving
MyConfig::GetInstance().SaveConfig();
```

\section preparing Preparing Config

You may want to set some default data when preparing new config, such as adding an item to the `items` array.

```cpp
// ----- myconfig.h -----

class MyConfigObj : public Config<MyConfig> {
    public:
        // ...
        void PrepareNewConfig() override;
};

// ----- myconfig.cpp -----

void MyConfig::PrepareNewConfig() {
    storedConfig.items.push_back("My item");
}
```

\section recreations Recreations

If a config fails to load, there is the option for recreating it. This allows you to implement the `Marmalade::Config::PrepareConfigRecreation()` and `Marmalade::Config::EndConfigRecreation()` methods to try and extract any data.

```cpp
void MyConfig::PrepareConfigRecreation() {
    // 1. Read file data
    // 2. Use non-JSON parsing techniques to find data, e.g. regex
    // 3. Save in a variable somewhere
}

void MyConfig::EndConfigRecreation() {
    // Modify storedConfig to set saved variable
}
```

\section migrations Migrations

If a key is added or removed in a new version of the config, the config wil fail to load.

Migrations allow you to fix the data before loading it into the structure.

```cpp
// ----- myconfig.h -----
#define CONFIG_VERSION 2

class MyConfigObj : public Config<MyConfig> {
    // ...
    private:
        static void migrateFromVersion1(nlohmann::json& data);
};

// ----- myconfig.cpp -----
MyConfigObj::MyConfigObj() : Config<MyConfig>(ConfigUtil::GetConfigDirectory() / "myconfig.json") {
    // ...
    version = CONFIG_VERSION;

    AddMigration(1, migrateFromVersion1);
}

void MyConfig::migrateFromVersion1(nlohmann::json& data) {
    // Add code here for adding or removing keys
}
```

If the version property in the config is lower than `CONFIG_VERSION`, the migrations will run, in order.

Note that the `data` provided here is not in a structure, it is a raw JSON object.

*/
